## 4. Tax and retirement terms for a business owner

If you're operating in the United States, these terms deserve particular attention.

| Term | What you need to understand |
| --- | --- |
| Self-employment tax | Social Security and Medicare taxes that may apply to self-employment income. |
| Pass-through taxation | Business income that generally flows through to owners' tax returns, depending on the entity and tax election. |
| S corporation election | A federal tax election that can change how qualifying business income and owner compensation are treated. It is not automatically beneficial for every business. |
| Traditional 401(k) or IRA | Retirement arrangements that can provide tax advantages, subject to eligibility and withdrawal rules. |
| Roth IRA or Roth 401(k) | Retirement arrangements funded with after-tax money, with qualified withdrawals generally tax-free. Eligibility and plan rules apply. |
| Solo 401(k) | A 401(k) arrangement for an eligible owner-only business, generally with no eligible common-law employees other than a spouse. |
| SEP IRA | A simplified employer retirement plan that can be used by eligible self-employed people and businesses. Employee eligibility rules matter. |
| Tax-loss harvesting | Selling investments at a loss to potentially offset capital gains, subject to tax rules such as wash-sale restrictions. |
| Qualified business income (QBI) deduction | A federal deduction potentially available to eligible taxpayers with qualifying business income, subject to limitations. |

The IRS explains retirement plan choices for self-employed people in its [official retirement-plan guide](<https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/retirement-plans-for-self-employed-people?utm_source=chatgpt.com>). The right option depends on your business structure, employees, income, and desired contribution level.

